WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:02.680
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to Deconstructing Chatbots.

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:05.090
I am Priyanka Vergadia,
and in this episode,

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:09.050
we will learn how to integrate
Dialogflow agent with ML APIs.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:12.252
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:19.830
Depending on what your
conversational AI use case is,

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:23.370
your users may want to upload an
image to the chat application,

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.950
and expect a response
that guides them further

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.930
in that conversation.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.220
This is exactly what
we will learn today.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.180
We will be seeing how to
create a Dialogflow agent that

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.380
fulfills an image request by
calling the Google Vision API.

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.530
We will be using the same Django
front-end application that we

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.920
created in the previous
episode and add image upload

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.270
functionality to it.

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:47.250
If you've not watched
the previous episode,

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:50.720
I highly recommend
you start there.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:51.220
All right.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:52.590
So here's what we are building.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:55.380
The user ends up on
our chat application

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.090
and interacts with it.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.940
The chatbot responds
with a greeting defining

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.500
the two things it can do--

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.459
setting up appointments
and understanding invoices.

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:07.500
We've already seen
setting up appointments

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.530
in the previous episodes, so
we'll just skip that for now

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:14.330
and just ask for
understanding our bill.

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:18.320
User is prompted to upload
the image of their invoice.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.950
Once they upload that image,
we display it on our front-end

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:25.090
and return back the text
detected in that image

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:28.280
by processing it
through the Vision API.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.860
Now, from architectural
standpoint,

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:32.570
here's how this is working.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:35.330
The chat app will be
served on App Engine.

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:38.240
The app is built in
Python using Django,

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.050
which is a Python-based free
and open source web framework.

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:45.740
App Engine will interact with
Cloud SQL for any database

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:46.670
requests.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:49.730
When users upload the
files, we will save them

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.620
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:54.080
And the app will interact
with our Dialogflow

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:57.380
agent via the Dialogflow API.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:59.540
The example uses the
appointment scheduler

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.770
that we created in
the third episode,

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.680
and enhanced in
the eighth episode,

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.350
to integrate with
Google Calendar.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.210
Today, we are
enhancing it further

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:11.030
by adding an integration
with Vision API.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.640
Now, with that background,
let's dive in and

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.710
see how to make it work.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:17.510
Check out the link in
the description below

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:20.840
to download the source
code for this application.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.180
Make sure to follow
the setup steps.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.070
I'm going to show you
the main part that

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:28.350
makes the integration with
the Vision API possible.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.500
But you can read the
elaborate setup instructions

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.360
in the readme file.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.750
First, let's look at
the Dialogflow console.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:39.230
I'm still using the previously
created appointment scheduler

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:41.810
agent from episode three.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:44.190
We will start from
the welcome intent.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:47.120
Modify the response so
that the users clearly

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.840
know that our chatbot can
handle appointment scheduling

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:54.250
and invoice-related questions.

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:56.470
Next, create an
intent to capture

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:59.320
users' input if they
are contacting us

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:00.610
about their invoice.

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:03.100
Provide a static
response telling the user

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.530
to upload a file.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:08.500
Create an intent that
takes the file input.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:11.050
Once the user has
provided the file,

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:14.890
we extract the file name
using a custom entity.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:17.020
If you don't know what
custom entities are,

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:18.670
check out episode six.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:22.180
And for the response,
enable the fulfillment.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.220
Check out the
front-end Django code

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:27.220
to see how to handle the
plumbing from front-end

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:28.540
to Dialogflow.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:31.610
Now, let's see the
fulfillment.js file.

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.210
Scroll down to the
intent map section,

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:38.800
and define the new intent
map for file upload.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:42.280
In the function
applyML pass the agent

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:45.880
and declare a variable to
get hold of the file name

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:48.220
that the user has uploaded.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:53.620
We can do that by writing
agent.parameters.filename.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:58.090
Then call a function to
handle Vision API request.

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:00.400
You pass the bucket
name and the file name

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.970
to create the GCS bucket pod.

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:08.140
Then you create the client
to call the Vision API.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.350
On this client, we call
the text detection method

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:16.070
and pass the GCS bucket pod from
where the file will be pulled.

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:18.769
At that point, we
have a resulting JSON

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:21.200
with all the detected text.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.000
Since for this
example, we are only

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.010
interested in the
text description,

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:27.800
I created a list of all
the text descriptions

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.570
and returned it as is.

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:33.110
Back in our intent map, we
are taking the return value

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:36.350
and sending it as a
response to the user.

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:38.690
As you can imagine,
the possibilities

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:41.390
are endless with an
integration like this.

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:43.760
Once you have it working,
you can pretty much

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:48.440
change the Vision API methods
to identify labels, landmarks,

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:50.970
faces, and more.

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:52.820
You can also use
the same concept

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:56.810
and integrate with other
ML APIs, like NLP, vision,

00:04:56.810 --> 00:05:01.100
translation, jobs, and
even your custom ML APIs

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.470
that you built through AutoML.

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:06.800
I cannot wait to hear from
you how you are applying this

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:07.700
integration.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:10.820
Tell us all about your use
case in the comments below.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:13.250
Today we learned how
to create a custom

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:17.330
Dialogflow front-end on App
Engine using Django framework

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:20.810
and integrated it with
Vision API on the back-end.

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:23.820
I hope to see you in the next
episode of Deconstructing

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:24.530
Chatbots.

